{"body":{"text":["Yeah, well, one thing about the context menu is just, it's not anything adds its entries, right? ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M88X8HVYZRC003"}," But in order for that to happen, of course, whatever is right-clicked on the hierarchy is available, right? So the menu has the hierarchy available and can do the expression work from there. So that has nothing to do with catching and allowing actions or side effects, etc. It's really just, there is an overlay system, the right-click has the position within the hierarchy that is naturally provided, and then the context menu component uses that simply. It does, to me, make sense to be able to, at the root, reserve gestures, etc. Otherwise, it's for the components. So in this case, I guess we're reserving the right-click, right, for the context menu. I don't see any reason to say that a URL and a collation is not the same thing. I mean, it's about the collation for our reader, it's about an expression. The URL is just a state mechanism, doesn't really matter to our discussion, I think. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M88X8HVYZRC004"}," That doesn't work. Components, whatever is mounted in a slot has to be agnostic decoupled. Now, one idea that seemed smart, seemed sane, was that cards aren't something a component is. A component is its content. And it's up to the parent to provide the card and the background if it is a card and a background. That means simply that the list is responsible. The problem then is nesting. So I don't know really. So then maybe it is so that the slot is to provide the Chrome, but it's a complicated thing. I can't quite grasp it yet. So no, I don't have any brilliant grounded ideas there. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M88X8HVYZRC002"}," I think, yeah, I'd appreciate agents try to say it's not the right idea. I mean, any idea should be scrutinized, but I think it makes sense. Maybe the overlay menu has a separation of collected items, like a label for the component and then its actions. And so then the labels show up the tree, and then maybe at the bottom there's a global action. So I don't know, but of course the overlay menu will need to accommodate what's needed for a good experience. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M88X8HVYZRC005"}," So we're definitely going to want to be able to do interesting canvas-based visualizations. And then there's a difference in the platform, such as DOM tree or canvas. Then it is something else that handles the data and deals with it on that platform, right? So I might have a canvas and then I might mount a specific visualization patch that takes a specific, as a component, right? It takes a specific kind of data and shows it accordingly. But again, I don't know how far we need to take that in terms of OpenLight's ideas. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M88X8HVYZRC006"}," That way, as you use the arrow keys in the completion menu and press enter and so on, it all just works. If there's a card layout, then you can go both maybe left and right to navigate it, all of these things. Maybe there's even a shortcut that you can do to search just on the actions that you have, not searching the page. So you see something that you want to click, then maybe you can do / write it, enter. These kinds of things, of course, should be possible. But then again, what implements accessibility might be up to the chassis, or might be a decoupled piece from the chassis. I don't know exactly. But the data that is needed to be able to build the experience should probably be shipped with the field itself, right? But that is also something I don't quite know yet, how it would work."]},"id":"1M88X8DXK610024","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
